WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
Speaker 1: What is forthcoming in terms&nbsp;
of criminal charges for former President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.180
Donald Trump. We are really getting into the&nbsp;
meat of this now as we see criminal referrals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:14.220
from the January six House Committee, the Georgia&nbsp;
investigation, the tax returns, possibly exposing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:20.280
tax fraud and evasion are coming out within days.&nbsp;
There is one opinion that is an interesting one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:26.700
which is from former Watergate prosecutor Nick&nbsp;
Ackerman, who expects Donald Trump to face both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:32.100
state and federal charges. What would happen&nbsp;
if that took place? We will discuss. But first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:38.400
let's look at what Nick Ackerman had to say on CNN&nbsp;
yesterday. And his prediction predictions are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.340
clear and they sound confident. Now, remember,&nbsp;
many times people say things confidently and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.900
they're wrong. So let's take that consideration.
Speaker 3: What do you think happens? That's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.620
everyone wants to know. Well, I charge Donald&nbsp;
Trump is going to be indicted. He's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.760
be indicted in Georgia, and I think he's going&nbsp;
to be indicted by the feds. All right. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.900
they've got the evidence. We don't know&nbsp;
exactly what a lot of these cooperating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:06.720
witnesses are saying. And I think that you've&nbsp;
got someone like Mark Meadows, who's probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.880
the weak link. And if you're looking for the&nbsp;
one witness, the most likely to turn, I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:18.840
vote him the most likely to turn. And what if so.
Speaker 1: If this happened, what happens in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:25.800
United States? Because this would be a very, very,&nbsp;
very big deal. If a former president were to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:32.340
indicted, there would be a number of issues that&nbsp;
would have to be overcome. First and foremost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:40.080
who sits on the jury. And as I've said before,&nbsp;
if you had someone like Trump in front of a jury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:48.480
how do you find a jury that is not going to be&nbsp;
subject to appeal because of the preconceived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:56.160
notions and biases of those jurors? There are very&nbsp;
few people in the United States who don't have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:02.940
strong opinion about Donald Trump, either for or&nbsp;
against. And if you find the people who genuinely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:12.600
don't have an opinion, they are usually acutely&nbsp;
and painfully uninformed people that I would also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.920
worry about having on a jury simply due to&nbsp;
their ability to understand what the hell is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.140
going on if I'm able to speak candidly with you.&nbsp;
So I do think that the jury question is a major,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:26.940
major question. But long before you even&nbsp;
got to that, it's important to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.820
the framework around criminal charges for a&nbsp;
president of the United States. First, you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:39.720
understand the legal immunity component. While the&nbsp;
president is president, there is significant legal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:45.840
immunity from prosecution for certain actions that&nbsp;
are taken as part of their official duties. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:52.860
are disagreements among the legal community, if&nbsp;
not connected to their presidential duties. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:57.960
president while president went and committed some&nbsp;
kind of cold blooded murder. Are they subject to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.640
prosecution? You can find legal opinions on&nbsp;
both sides of that question, but that's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:08.220
actually what we're talking about right now.&nbsp;
And that immunity extends to certain actions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:15.300
taken after a president leaves office. But how&nbsp;
far that goes is also a subject of legal debate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:21.060
There are political considerations because the&nbsp;
prosecution and theoretically the conviction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:27.480
of a former president on criminal charges is&nbsp;
undoubtedly going to be a politicized event.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:33.180
And that can make it extraordinarily difficult for&nbsp;
prosecutors to actually get a conviction. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.260
you do get one, you could have serious problems,&nbsp;
as I mentioned, in terms of who was on that jury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:44.880
and the jury selection. And it's very finding&nbsp;
someone guilty in the court of public opinion is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.080
a very different thing than finding legal guilt&nbsp;
beyond a reasonable beyond a reasonable doubt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:54.180
In a criminal court, you also have a burden of&nbsp;
proof issue when going after a former president.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:59.940
You have that in general. But in order to get&nbsp;
a conviction on criminal charges, prosecutors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:05.940
do need to establish the defendant's guilt beyond&nbsp;
that reasonable doubt. In general, that's a tough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:11.400
standard in the American legal system. That's why&nbsp;
we have many acquittals. But when the defendant is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:20.280
a former president with access to every resource&nbsp;
and advisers and the best lawyers, that can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:26.640
be a significant challenge to overcome. And also&nbsp;
these things move slowly. Trump has been out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:33.480
office now for just short of two years, and the&nbsp;
passage of time can make it more difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:40.260
secure convictions on criminal charges if there is&nbsp;
a lot of distance from when the alleged crime took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:48.180
place. And this can be because of inability to&nbsp;
locate witnesses who remember relevant issues or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:53.700
a loss of evidence or for a whole number of other&nbsp;
reasons. And also, it's important to consider that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:59.100
public sentiment can change. George W Bush is a&nbsp;
good example of this, and I've been careful on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:06.120
this probe. Graham never to rehabilitate George&nbsp;
W Bush's image while acknowledging like, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:12.120
he's not as whacked as Trump in a lot of different&nbsp;
ways. Right after George W Bush left office. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:16.200
you were to pull the American public, I think&nbsp;
you would have found that there was significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:23.520
support for going after Bush as a war criminal for&nbsp;
his actions related to the Iraq war. My belief is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:28.200
and this is just speculation and maybe there's&nbsp;
polling on this, I don't know that if you pulled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:34.440
the country now on that exact same question, you&nbsp;
would find much less support for going after Bush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:41.400
for war crimes, even though the actions taken&nbsp;
a while he was president don't differ. Now this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:46.980
is different than was he immune because these&nbsp;
were actions taken in Iraq related to his work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:51.060
It's not it's not even a legal question. It's&nbsp;
just a public opinion question. I believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:57.000
although the facts haven't changed since George&nbsp;
W Bush left office, the interest from the public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:02.580
in going after Bush for whatever wrongdoing he&nbsp;
may have participated in would have been greatly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:07.620
diminished. And I do think that the longer that&nbsp;
this goes without charges against Trump, that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:12.420
potentially also going to be a factor. So Nick&nbsp;
Ackerman says legal criminal charges coming at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:18.600
the state and federal level. We will probably soon&nbsp;
know as so many things are now happening at once

